# Analects Book 16 (季氏) — draft, second part: lemmata 13–34 = passages 16.2–16.14 (UNITS_B[0] is lemma 13).
# The running head 論語巻八 (lemma 35) is skipped by the build and not drafted.
# Token format as the pilot:
#   tokens separated by " | "; a token is  CHARS[PUNCT][=pinyin][/context gloss[//char gloss//char gloss]]
#   a leading "^" breaks the verse line before the token.
# Punctuation is editorial (the edition has none) and always trails the word before it.
# Pinyin defaults to the glossary's first reading; every polyphone states its reading here, and so does
# every bound compound and every character not yet in the glossary (on every occurrence).
# Passages follow Zhu Xi's chapter breaks (凡十四章; each ○ checked in the commentary file): 16.2 = units 13–15,
# 16.3 = 16, 16.4 = 17, 16.5 = 18, 16.6 = 19, 16.7 = 20, 16.8 = 21–22, 16.9 = 23, 16.10 = 24, 16.11 = 25–26,
# 16.12 = 27–28, 16.13 = 29–33, 16.14 = 34. Each stretch holds at most one chapter (no npas).
# Cross-references cite the shipped passage numbers (Zhu Xi's chapters, as the cards show them: Boyi and Shuqi
#   at 5.22 and 7.14, 我非生而知之者 at 7.19, 見利思義 at 14.13, 到于今 at 14.18).
# Quotation marks balance within every unit. Where a speech runs on past one of Zhu Xi's comments the
# continuation is left unquoted, Chinese and English alike (as Books 8–15): 16.2 units 14–15 (Kongzi), 16.8
# unit 22 (Kongzi), 16.11 unit 26 (Kongzi), 16.13 units 31–32 (Bo Yu). Quote inside a quote: 『』 in w, ‘’ in
# l and i (25, the old saying; 30, the Master's words inside Bo Yu's answer). In the unquoted continuation 31
# the Master's and Bo Yu's words inside it take first-level marks 「」/“ ”. The saying in 26 (隠居以求其志，
# 行義以達其道) opens its unit, where the token grammar allows no leading mark, so it is left unmarked and
# the colon after it carries the sense. 16.12 (27–28) has no speaker; Zhu Xi thinks 孔子曰 has dropped out, so
# it is printed without quotation marks. 16.14 (34) has no speaker either; the titles are given without marks
# in w and in quotation marks in l/i.
# Repeats (shipped English reused): 天下有道 / 無道 'the world has / lacks the Way' (13–15; 8.13); 禮樂 'the
#   rites and music', 樂 yuè (13; 11.1, 13.3); 十世 'ten generations' unbound (13; 2.23 has 十世 'ten ages' in
